  "response": "Here's a Python function that reverses the order of elements and their associated punctuation marks while preserving their pairing by position:\n\n```python\ndef reverse_list_elements(elements, punctuation):\n    \"\"\"\n    Reverses the order of elements and punctuation while keeping each punctuation \n    mark attached to its original element based on position.\n\n    Args:\n        elements (list): List of string elements\n        punctuation (list): List of punctuation marks corresponding to elements\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: (reversed_elements, reversed_punctuation)\n    \"\"\"\n    # Reverse both lists and return as a tuple\n    reversed_elements = elements[::-1]\n    reversed_punctuation = punctuation[::-1]\n\n    return reversed_elements, reversed_punctuation\n```\n\n### Example Usage\n\n```python\n# Example 1\nelements1 = [\"Hello\", \"world\"]\npunctuation1 = [\"\", \"!\"]\nprint(reverse_list_elements(elements1, punctuation1))  \n# Output: (['world', 'Hello'], ['!', ''])\n\n# Example 2\nelements2 = [\"Hi\", \"There!\", \"Bye!!\"]\npunctuation2 = [\".\", \"!\", \".\"]\nprint(reverse_list_elements(elements2, punctuation2))  \n# Output: (['Bye!!', 'There!', 'Hi'], ['.', '!', '.'])\n\n# Example 3\nelements3 = []\npunctuation3 = []\nprint(reverse_list_elements(elements3, punctuation3))  \n# Output: ([], [])\n```\n\nThis function ensures that each element remains associated with its punctuation by reversing both lists in parallel. It also gracefully handles edge cases such as empty input lists.",
